WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.550
While we wait for official Avengers 4 news
from the notoriously tight-lipped crew at

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:08.800
Marvel, here's a look at why Captain America
is crucial to the outcome of the war against

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.360
Thanos and whether he'll still be standing
when those end credits roll.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.280
The biggest news to break regarding Captain
America since the epic final moments of Infinity

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.820
War came to us in the form of a quick goodbye
note tweeted by none other than Chris Evans

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:22.360
himself.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.500
In only a few heartfelt words, Evans let the
whole world know that he would be putting

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.070
down his shield for good.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:31.869
The tweet, which amassed several hundred thousand
likes within hours, read:

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:33.880
"Officially wrapped on Avengers 4.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.980
It was an emotional day to say the least.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.960
Playing this role over the last 8 years has
been an honor.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.630
To everyone in front of the camera, behind
the camera, and in the audience, thank you

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:43.630
for the memories!

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:44.420
Eternally grateful."

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:48.980
Maybe we'll see a cameo or two down the road,
but, for all intents and purposes, it does

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.380
seem like this is the end for Steve Rogers.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.800
Infinity War may have been one of the wildest
rides in cinema history, but Captain America's

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.690
role in it was actually fairly minor.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:02.910
Sure, we got a fun "re-emergence" sequence
for Cap in the fight at the train station

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:07.170
in Edinburgh, and his attempt to rally a crew
of heroes together to defend the world against

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.180
bad guys was a crucial part of the plot.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.560
But even if Captain America ultimately received
a decent amount of screen time, it did feel

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:17.290
a bit like he was being used to move the story
forward more than anything else.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.740
“Earth just lost her best defender.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:20.900
So we’re here to fight.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.100
And if you want to stand in our way, we’ll
fight you too.”

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.159
The truth is, Captain America ended up having
a muted role in Infinity War.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:34.060
The story had him stranded in the middle of
a sidebar conflict in Wakanda, while Iron

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.680
Man took front and center on Titan.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.780
It stands to reason that this will set Steve
Rogers up to be a crucial element in the next

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:43.070
chapter, where we'll almost certainly see
more of Cap's storyline unfold.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:48.040
Captain America: Civil War ended on a pretty
low note, and many fans were hoping for some

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:52.549
onscreen reconciliation between Steve Rogers
and Tony Stark in Infinity War.

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:54.920
In the end, the two didn't even share a scene.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.460
“The Avengers broke up.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:56.960
We’re toast.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:58.640
Broke up?

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:01.300
Like a band?

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.620
like the Beatles?”

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:05.740
That's not to say that the folks behind the
scenes didn't try to fit it in.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.979
Writer Stephen McFeely explained that in the
original drafts of the script, they had Steve

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:10.599
and Tony in the same room together.

00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:12.069
Ultimately, however, he said:

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:15.950
"As many times as we wrote those scenes, it
became clear that this movie needed to be

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:19.700
propulsive and be about Thanos and what he
represented to the Avengers."

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.659
It may not have fit into Infinity War’s
plot, but there's no way these two aren't

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.580
sharing some screen time in Avengers 4.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.220
Their long-anticipated reconciliation will
no doubt be a pivotal scene and one that,

00:02:30.239 --> 00:02:32.329
sadly, paves the way for Cap's demise.

00:02:32.329 --> 00:02:35.760
Because once that breach is healed, Cap's
character arc will be nearly complete.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.700
Just like a few of the other key players in
the MCU, Captain America's story arc is in

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:41.900
desperate need of closure.

00:02:42.029 --> 00:02:46.419
We've watched him rise up to lead the Avengers
before entering into a self-imposed exile,

00:02:46.419 --> 00:02:50.430
only to resurface for Infinity War but the
character is still in need of a final act

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:51.740
to bring his story to an end.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.840
The Russo brothers have been quiet about what
might happen after the third Avengers, only

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.049
suggesting that Infinity War was, quote:

00:02:58.049 --> 00:03:01.650
"...the next chapter, so to speak, in his
specific story.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.709
And there's been no talk about where we go
beyond that yet."

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:08.849
That comment could quite easily be used to
infer that after Infinity War there just isn't

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:11.860
much more left for Steve Rogers to do in the
MCU.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:14.100
One way or another, he needs some closure.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:20.600
“Family, stability the guy who wanted that
went into the ice 75 years ago.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.740
I think someone else came out.”

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.340
The truth is, Steve Rogers doesn't really
have to die in Avengers 4 for his story arc

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:30.760
to wrap up, for Chris Evans to move on, and
for the universe to be saved.

00:03:30.969 --> 00:03:34.680
The only thing that seems inevitable is that
the character won't be with us on a regular

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.599
basis during Phase 4 of the MCU.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.299
How could this happen without a heart-wrenching
death scene?

00:03:39.299 --> 00:03:43.849
Rogers just needs to pass the Captain America
torch on to a successor, which could just

00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:47.620
as easily take place after an epic comeback
victory against the Mad Titan.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:51.360
There's a lot to be said for quitting while
you’re ahead and how much further ahead

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:52.879
do you get than defeating Thanos?

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:56.949
There's no shortage of rumors floating about
regarding who could replace Steve Rogers as

00:03:56.949 --> 00:03:57.660
Captain America.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:02.820
Whether it's Bucky Barnes, Sam Wilson or even
a complete wildcard, chances are the mantle

00:04:02.829 --> 00:04:05.059
of Captain America will be passed on.

00:04:05.059 --> 00:04:08.829
The question is, will Steve Rogers live to
see the passing of the torch... or will it

00:04:08.829 --> 00:04:11.659
be wrenched from his grasp by Thanos' hands?

